WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.071
Merchant M-0042's ledger takes updates from one worker at a time.

2
00:00:08.071 --> 00:00:13.359
Workers A and B run the same code, so B can take over if A dies.

3
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:17.193
They agree through a lease: a lock that runs out on its own.

4
00:00:17.193 --> 00:00:22.391
A holds it two seconds at a time, and renews it whenever less than one second is left.

5
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:31.491
Every hundred milliseconds, A checks that its lease is still valid, spends about sixty milliseconds building an update, and writes it to the ledger.

6
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.134
At fifteen hundred milliseconds, A checks: one point six seconds left.

7
00:00:37.134 --> 00:00:39.077
It starts building the update.

8
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:43.738
Twenty milliseconds later, a garbage collection pause stops A's thread.

9
00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:47.643
For three seconds none of A's code runs, not even the renewal.

10
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.244
The world keeps going.

11
00:00:49.244 --> 00:00:54.887
A little after three seconds, lease one runs out, and the service grants lease two to B.

12
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:59.063
B starts writing, and thirteen of its updates land on the ledger.

13
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:02.684
At about four and a half seconds, A's thread runs again.

14
00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:04.514
From inside, nothing happened.

15
00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:11.959
It was in the middle of building the update, so it finishes and writes it, one point four seconds after its lease ran out.

16
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.860
The check was true when it ran.

17
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.763
The pause landed between the check and the write, and the write went ahead on an answer three seconds old.

18
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:21.569
Could A notice the pause?

19
00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:29.132
Its own clock jumps by three seconds, but A only sees that on its next reading, after the write is already on its way.

20
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.782
Checking again right before the write shrinks the gap to a few milliseconds.

21
00:01:33.782 --> 00:01:37.143
But a pause can still land right after that second check.

22
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:40.143
From inside, a program runs without a break.

23
00:01:40.143 --> 00:01:43.614
From outside, it can stop between any two instructions.

24
00:01:43.614 --> 00:01:46.895
Stopping the stale write takes help from the ledger.

25
00:01:46.895 --> 00:01:48.977
That is the next lesson, fencing.
